    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-216704",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043589/assets/91f4aac82208a2801652c9112f58518e1abc89ed4313601a60f7c183b7757f70.png)\n\nFind the $10$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above.",
      "worked_solution": "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{10}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/10}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043589/assets/d0fc6866d4bb06ec6cbf20bb719faf722ec3c1efe3fba03e413f51dc1c827e71.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=8.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{8}={e}^{(8)\\pi \\text{i}/5}={e}^{8\\pi \\text{i}/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
